postrophe.","D":"The possessive apostrophe is missing."}}},{"id":"69cfca07-11a8-5773-a70a-aab04b7f5c0a","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual children: “two ____”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"childrenes"},{"id":"B","text":"children"},{"id":"C","text":"child''s"},{"id":"D","text":"children''"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Look at the number **two**, then recall the noun’s plural; an apostrophe is not a plural ending.","explanation":"The ordinary plural of **child** is **children**. The number two requires that plural sense.","keywords":["plural-form"],"skill":"plural-form","contextKey":"nouns-and-plurals-4","fingerprint":"a45601e2ae965c118af6ca55e4513fa2d2a39044a64aadbabc8e503c45cb444d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Look at the number **two**, then recall the noun’s plural; an apostrophe is not a plural ending.","The ordinary plural of **child** is **children**. The number two requires that plural sense.","Choose **children**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This adds an extra ending to the established plural form.","B":"The ordinary plural of **child** is **children**. The number two requires that plural sense.","C":"Apostrophe + s marks possession, not a plural count.","D":"A possessive apostrophe is not needed to count these nouns."}}},{"id":"d6c18d63-91e7-56e2-a2d2-cb4a34f5fef0","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Choose the verb: “These children ____ easy to recognise.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"be"},{"id":"B","text":"are"},{"id":"C","text":"is"},{"id":"D","text":"am"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The demonstrative **these** makes the plural number explicit.","explanation":"**These children** is plural, so use **are**.","keywords":["plura