the wrong possessive ending."}}},{"id":"4f226f9a-aa6e-5f6d-a453-2a1b91cb233e","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual cups: “two ____”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"cupses"},{"id":"B","text":"cups"},{"id":"C","text":"cup''s"},{"id":"D","text":"cups''"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Look at the number **two**, then recall the noun’s plural; an apostrophe is not a plural ending.","explanation":"The ordinary plural of **cup** is **cups**. The number two requires that plural sense.","keywords":["plural-form"],"skill":"plural-form","contextKey":"nouns-and-plurals-50","fingerprint":"649ce415942bde143a1ffe3edd5c45867f592488805b552391518e893c30a233","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Look at the number **two**, then recall the noun’s plural; an apostrophe is not a plural ending.","The ordinary plural of **cup** is **cups**. The number two requires that plural sense.","Choose **cups**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This adds an extra ending to the established plural form.","B":"The ordinary plural of **cup** is **cups**. The number two requires that plural sense.","C":"Apostrophe + s marks possession, not a plural count.","D":"A possessive apostrophe is not needed to count these nouns."}}},{"id":"5a5f40b6-9940-599c-a432-644c3d436c14","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Choose the verb: “These cups ____ easy to recognise.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"am"},{"id":"B","text":"be"},{"id":"C","text":"are"},{"id":"D","text":"is"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The demonstrative **these** makes the plural number explicit.","explanation":"**These cups** is plural, so use **are**.","keywords":["plural-agreement"],"skill":"plural-agreement","contextKey":"nouns-and-plurals-50","fingerprint":"f3c050b6e6d736ca0dceb7f0177033b98a086b2c5d62edb52bbb5bfc9efd4764","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The demonstrative **these** makes the plural number explicit.","**These cups** is plural, so use **are**.","Choose **are**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Am agrees with I, not this plural noun phrase.","B":"Be is not the finite present form required here.","C":"**These cups** is plural, so use **are**.","D":"Is is singular and does not agree with these."}}},{"id":"1392626a-3ad1-54a6-a564-0bfe39c898b7","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Point to one item: “____ cup is in the picture.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"This"},{"id":"B","text":"These"},{"id":"C","text":"Those"},{"id":"D","text":"Many"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Match the demonstrative to **one** item and the singular verb is.","explanation":"The instruction specifies one cup, so the singular demonstrative is **this**.","keywords":["singular-demonstrative"],"skill":"singular-demonstrative","contextKey":"nouns-and-plurals-50","fingerprint":"b7702171032b3df2156d2bfe4df8ad687e1282576e4be23bb21b8299762aa93f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Match the demonstrative to **one** item and the singular verb is.","The instruction specifies one cup, so the singular demonstrative is **this**.","Choose **This**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The instruction specifies one cup, so the singular demonstrative is **this**.","B":"These is plural, but the noun phrase refers to one item.","C":"Those is plural.","D":"Many requires a plural count noun and would not agree with is."}}},{"id":"87901e9c-a97b-5e29-ae07-9b1d8545f43c","topicSlug":"nouns-and-plurals","level":"A1","prompt":"Several cups share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “cups”.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"cups"},{"id":"B","text":"cupss''s"},{"id":"C","text":"cups''s''"},{"id":"D","text":"cups''"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Find the plural noun’s final letter, then choose **apostrophe only** or **apostrophe + s**.","explanation":"The plural **cups** already ends in s, so add only an apostrophe.","keywords":["plural-possession"],"skill":"plural-possession","contextKey":"nouns-and-plurals-50","fingerprint":"37d10ecfc3a98b87029f738074c9db20e1ab4d71be83964cfa4e8ba567f228f0","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-nouns"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find the plural noun’s final letter, then choose **apostrophe only** or **apostrophe + s**.","The plural **cups** already ends in s, so add only an apostrophe.","Choose **cups''**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The possessive apostrophe is missing.","B":"This adds an extra plural-like s and the wrong possessive ending.","C":"Two apostrophes are not used for this possessive.","D":"The plural **cups** already ends in s, so add only an apostrophe."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Recognise separate things, form their plurals and make nearby words agree with their number.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Regular plural endings","explanation":"Most countable nouns form a plural with **-s**. After endings such as -s, -sh, -ch and -x, we often add **-es**. After a consonant + y, replace y with **-ies**. After a vowel + y, keep y and add -s. The ending changes the noun’s number, not its ownership.","pattern":"book**s** · box**es** · cit**ies** · key**s**","commonMistakes":["An apostrophe does not normally make a plural: two books, not two book’s."],"examples":["Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual books: “two **books**”.","Choose the ordinary plural phrase for individual boxes: “two **boxes**”."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Irregular and unchanged plurals","explanation":"Some nouns change internally: man → men, tooth → teeth. Others have the same singular and plural form, such as sheep and species. Learn each noun with its plural. A word ending in -s is not automatically plural: news usually takes singular agreement.","pattern":"one **child**, two **children** · one **sheep**, two **sheep**","commonMistakes":["Fish is a common plural for individual fish; fishes can refer to different kinds. The practice specifies the ordinary count sense."],"examples":["Choose the verb: “These books **are** easy to recognise.”","Choose the verb: “These boxes **are** easy to recognise.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Agreement around the noun","explanation":"Use **this / that / a / an** with a singular count noun and **these / those** with a plural. A plural noun used as subject normally takes a plural verb. The words around an unchanged noun can reveal its number.","pattern":"**These sheep are** calm. / **This sheep is** calm.","commonMistakes":["This books mixes a singular demonstrative with a plural noun."],"examples":["Point to one item: “**This** book is in the picture.”","Point to one item: “**This** box is in the picture.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Plural form is not possession","explanation":"Distinguish plural -s from possessive ’s and s’. A plural noun says how many; a possessive marks a relationship. In two students’ bags, students is plural and the apostrophe marks possession. With an irregular plural, add ’s: children’s.","pattern":"two **students** · one **student’s** bag · two **students’** bags","commonMistakes":["Some nouns such as furniture are uncountable in their usual sense. Use pieces of furniture, not furnitures."],"examples":["Several books share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “books”. → **books''**","Several boxes share the belongings. Choose the possessive spelling for the plural noun “boxes”. → **boxes''**"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["book**s** · box**es** · cit**ies** · key**s**","one **child**, two **children** · one **sheep**, two **sheep**","**These sheep are** calm. / **This sheep is** calm.","two **students** · one **student’s** bag · two **students’** bags"]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["sentence-structure"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
